{% if not suppress_answer %}{% include "optimization_answer.html" %}{% endif %}
{% if opt.ok %}
<div class="opt-highlight card">
  <div class="card-sub">Best-supported measured backend for the selected preferences</div>
  <div class="opt-winner">{{ opt.winner.id }}{% if opt.answer %} <span class="rb-friendly">{{ opt.answer.winner }}</span>{% endif %}</div>
  {% if opt.tie and opt.tie.is_tie %}<p class="rb-tienote" role="note"><span class="rb-tag rb-tag-T" aria-hidden="true">T</span> Tied with <b>{{ opt.tie.others|join(', ') }}</b> — shown first because of {{ opt.tie.reason }} (tie rule).{% if opt.tie.all_tied %} All {{ opt.tie.ids|length }} eligible backends have the same score under these priorities.{% endif %}</p>{% endif %}
  <div class="opt-kpis">
    <div><b>{{ '%.5f'|format(opt.winner.runtime_s) }} s</b><span>Inference runtime</span></div>
    <div><b>{{ '%.1f'|format(opt.winner.memory_mb) }} MB</b><span>Peak process RSS (growth {{ '%.1f'|format(opt.winner.memory_growth_mb) }} MB)</span></div>
    <div><b>{{ '%.3f'|format(opt.winner.scaling_k) }}</b><span>Scaling exponent k</span></div>
    <div><b>{{ ('%.2f'|format(opt.winner.accuracy_pct) ~ '%') if opt.winner.accuracy_pct is not none else 'Not measured' }}</b><span>Native MNIST accuracy*</span></div>
  </div>
  <p class="opt-small"><b>{{ 'Exploratory four-criterion scenario' if opt.exploratory else 'Primary three-criterion scenario' }}.</b> Weights actually used: inference {{ '%.1f'|format(opt.weights.inference*100) }}%, memory {{ '%.1f'|format(opt.weights.memory*100) }}%, scalability {{ '%.1f'|format(opt.weights.scalability*100) }}%, accuracy {{ '%.1f'|format(opt.weights.accuracy*100) }}%. Lower score is preferred under these particular assumptions, not universally. {{ opt.message if opt.exploratory else 'Accuracy is displayed for context but does not influence this recommendation.' }}</p>
</div>
{% set ST = opt.stability %}
{% if ST %}
<div class="card rb-stability" id="stabilityNote">
  <div class="card-title">Stability of this recommendation</div>
  <ul class="rb-checks">
    {% for c in ST.checks %}<li class="rb-{{ c.status }}"><span class="rb-mark" aria-hidden="true">{{ {'same':'✓','changes':'✗','becomes_tie':'≈','resolves':'≈','n/a':'–'}[c.status] }}</span> {{ c.text }}</li>{% endfor %}
    {% if ST.margin %}<li class="rb-margin"><span class="rb-mark" aria-hidden="true">Δ</span> Margin to runner-up ({{ ST.margin.runner_up }}): <b>{{ '%.4f'|format(ST.margin.value) }}</b> ({{ ST.margin.note }})</li>{% endif %}
  </ul>
  <p class="opt-small">{{ ST.note }} <a href="/robustness">Robustness page</a></p>
</div>
{% endif %}
<div class="opt-two-charts">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Weighted ranking: where each score comes from</div>
    <div class="card-sub">Shorter stacked bars are better. Each segment is that criterion's normalised cost multiplied by its relative weight; not an absolute performance percentage.</div>
    <p class="rb-sowhat"><b>In plain words:</b> a shorter bar is a better overall score. The colours show which criterion costs each backend the most.</p>
    <div class="opt-legend">
      {% for name, label in [('inference','Inference'),('memory','Memory'),('scalability','Scalability'),('accuracy','Accuracy*')] %}<span><i class="opt-dot opt-{{ name }}"></i>{{ label }}</span>{% endfor %}
    </div>
    <div class="opt-bars" role="img" aria-label="Weighted cost comparison bar chart by simulator backend">
      {% for r in opt.rows %}
      <div class="opt-bar-row" title="{{ r.id }} · weighted score {{ '%.4f'|format(r.score) }}">
        <div class="opt-bar-label">{{ loop.index }}. {{ r.id }}{% if loop.first %} ★{% endif %}</div>
        <div class="opt-bar-track">
          {% for key in ['inference','memory','scalability','accuracy'] %}
          <div class="opt-segment opt-{{ key }}" style="width:{{ '%.3f'|format(r.contributions[key]*100) }}%" title="{{ key }} contribution: {{ '%.4f'|format(r.contributions[key]) }}"></div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
        <strong class="opt-bar-score">{{ '%.3f'|format(r.score) }}</strong>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Inference versus memory: measured trade-offs</div>
    <div class="card-sub">Measured runtime and peak process memory of each eligible backend, both on logarithmic axes. ★ is the weighted recommendation; outlined points are Pareto-efficient on the active criteria. The plot shows two observed quantities only: the score uses memory <em>growth</em> rather than the absolute footprint, and also uses scaling and, if enabled, accuracy.</div>
    <p class="rb-sowhat"><b>In plain words:</b> towards the lower left means faster and a smaller memory footprint in absolute terms. Outlined points are Pareto-efficient on the criteria in use, which include memory growth and scaling, not only these two axes.</p>
    <svg class="opt-scatter" viewBox="0 0 650 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatterplot of measured inference runtime against peak process memory">
      {% for t in opt.plot.yticks %}
      <line x1="{{ opt.plot.x0 }}" x2="{{ opt.plot.x1 }}" y1="{{ t.pos }}" y2="{{ t.pos }}" stroke="#e2e9f3"/>
      <text x="{{ opt.plot.x0 - 6 }}" y="{{ t.pos + 4 }}" font-size="11" fill="#53657c" text-anchor="end">{{ t.label }}</text>
      {% endfor %}
      {% for t in opt.plot.xticks %}
      <line x1="{{ t.pos }}" x2="{{ t.pos }}" y1="{{ opt.plot.y1 }}" y2="{{ opt.plot.y0 }}" stroke="#eef2f8"/>
      <text x="{{ t.pos }}" y="{{ opt.plot.y0 + 16 }}" font-size="11" fill="#53657c" text-anchor="middle">{{ t.label }}</text>
      {% endfor %}
      <line x1="{{ opt.plot.x0 }}" x2="{{ opt.plot.x1 }}" y1="{{ opt.plot.y0 }}" y2="{{ opt.plot.y0 }}" stroke="#65758c" stroke-width="1.2"/>
      <line x1="{{ opt.plot.x0 }}" x2="{{ opt.plot.x0 }}" y1="{{ opt.plot.y1 }}" y2="{{ opt.plot.y0 }}" stroke="#65758c" stroke-width="1.2"/>
      {% for r in opt.rows %}
      <g>
        <title>{{ r.id }}: {{ '%.5f'|format(r.runtime_s) }} s; {{ '%.1f'|format(r.memory_mb) }} MB peak RSS; growth {{ '%.1f'|format(r.memory_growth_mb) }} MB; weighted score {{ '%.3f'|format(r.score) }}</title>
        {% if loop.first %}<circle cx="{{ r.plot_x }}" cy="{{ r.plot_y }}" r="11" fill="none" stroke="#d69c22" stroke-width="3"/>{% endif %}
        <circle cx="{{ r.plot_x }}" cy="{{ r.plot_y }}" r="{{ 6 if r.pareto else 4.5 }}" fill="{{ theme.colors.get(r.framework,'#64748b') }}" stroke="{{ '#223147' if r.pareto else 'white' }}" stroke-width="1.3"/>
        <text x="{{ r.label_x }}" y="{{ r.label_y }}" font-size="10.5" fill="#31435e" text-anchor="{{ r.label_anchor }}">{{ r.id }}{% if loop.first %} ★{% endif %}</text>
      </g>
      {% endfor %}
      <text x="{{ (opt.plot.x0 + opt.plot.x1) / 2 }}" y="292" font-size="12" fill="#31435e" text-anchor="middle">Inference runtime at N={{ '{:,}'.format(opt.network_size) }} (logarithmic)</text>
      <text transform="translate(16,145) rotate(-90)" font-size="12" fill="#31435e" text-anchor="middle">Peak process memory (MB, log)</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="opt-small">Highlighted circle: current preferred backend. Point colours follow the simulator legend used elsewhere in the dashboard. Hover for the exact figures.</p>
  </div>
</div>
{% set P = opt.profiles %}
{% if P %}
<div class="card" id="selectionNetwork">
  <div class="card-title">Selection network: backends → measured criteria → reader profiles</div>
  <div class="card-sub">Each output node is one reader profile and computes a weighted sum of the normalised criteria, <i>y</i> = <i>W</i><i>x</i>. Lines from a backend are darker where it does better on that criterion; lines into a profile are thicker where that profile gives the criterion more weight. The arrow names the backend each profile would choose at N={{ '{:,}'.format(opt.network_size) }} ({{ 'share-of-total' if opt.normalisation == 'share' else 'min–max' }} normalisation). Profiles are illustrative readers, not elicited preferences; * marks profiles that use the exploratory accuracy criterion.</div>
  <p class="rb-sowhat"><b>In plain words:</b> each profile is a different kind of reader. The table below lists every backend's score for every profile; the highlighted cell is that profile's choice.</p>
  {% set nb = P.backends|length %}{% set npf = P.profiles|length %}
  {% set H = 40 + 34 * ([nb, npf, 4]|max) %}
  {% set yb = (H - 34 * (nb - 1)) / 2 %}{% set yp = (H - 34 * (npf - 1)) / 2 %}{% set yc = (H - 70 * 3) / 2 %}
  {% set cy = {'inference': yc, 'memory': yc + 70, 'scalability': yc + 140, 'accuracy': yc + 210} %}
  {% set clab = {'inference': 'Inference speed', 'memory': 'Memory growth', 'scalability': 'Scalability', 'accuracy': 'Accuracy*'} %}
  <svg class="opt-network" viewBox="0 0 1180 {{ H }}" role="img" aria-label="Selection network diagram">
    {% for b in P.backends %}{% set by = yb + 34 * loop.index0 %}{% set fw = b.split('/')[0] %}
      {% for c in P.criteria %}{% set g = P.goodness.get(b, {}).get(c) %}{% if g is not none %}
      <line x1="232" y1="{{ by }}" x2="488" y2="{{ cy[c] }}" stroke="{{ theme.colors.get(fw, '#64748b') }}" stroke-opacity="{{ '%.2f'|format(0.08 + 0.62 * g) }}" stroke-width="1.6"><title>{{ b }} on {{ clab[c] }}: relative performance {{ '%.2f'|format(g) }} (1 = best eligible)</title></line>
      {% endif %}{% endfor %}
    {% endfor %}
    {% for pf in P.profiles %}{% set py = yp + 34 * loop.index0 %}
      {% for c in P.criteria %}{% set w = pf.weights[c] %}{% if w > 0 %}
      <line x1="512" y1="{{ cy[c] }}" x2="788" y2="{{ py }}" stroke="#2f5f86" stroke-opacity="0.55" stroke-width="{{ '%.1f'|format(1 + 7 * w) }}"><title>{{ pf.label }}: weight {{ '%.0f'|format(100 * w) }}% on {{ clab[c] }}</title></line>
      {% endif %}{% endfor %}
    {% endfor %}
    {% for b in P.backends %}{% set by = yb + 34 * loop.index0 %}{% set fw = b.split('/')[0] %}
      <circle cx="232" cy="{{ by }}" r="9" fill="{{ theme.colors.get(fw, '#64748b') }}"/>
      <text x="216" y="{{ by + 4 }}" font-size="13" fill="#24364f" text-anchor="end">{{ b }}</text>
    {% endfor %}
    {% for c in P.criteria %}
      <circle cx="500" cy="{{ cy[c] }}" r="13" fill="#eef4fa" stroke="#2f5f86" stroke-width="2"/>
      <text x="500" y="{{ cy[c] - 20 }}" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#24364f" text-anchor="middle">{{ clab[c] }}</text>
    {% endfor %}
    {% for pf in P.profiles %}{% set py = yp + 34 * loop.index0 %}{% set win = P.winners.get(pf.key) %}
      <circle cx="800" cy="{{ py }}" r="10" fill="{{ '#fdf3e1' if pf.exploratory else '#e9f5ee' }}" stroke="{{ '#a86b12' if pf.exploratory else '#2e7d5b' }}" stroke-width="2"/>
      <text x="818" y="{{ py + 4 }}" font-size="13" fill="#24364f"><tspan font-weight="700">{{ pf.label }}</tspan> → {{ P.labels[pf.key] if P.labels else (win if win else 'no difference (all tied)') }}</text>
    {% endfor %}
  </svg>
  <div class="opt-scroll"><table class="opt-table opt-profile-table"><thead><tr><th>Simulator / backend</th>{% for pf in P.profiles %}<th>{{ pf.label }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
  <tbody>{% for b in P.backends %}<tr><td><b>{{ b }}</b></td>{% for pf in P.profiles %}{% set cell = P.scores.get(b, {}).get(pf.key) %}{% set tied_here = (P.ties[pf.key].ids if P.ties else []) %}<td class="{{ 'opt-row-winner' if P.winners.get(pf.key) == b else '' }}{{ ' rb-tied' if b in tied_here else '' }}">{% if cell %}{{ '%.3f'|format(cell.score) }} <span class="opt-small">(#{{ cell.rank }})</span>{% if b in tied_here %} <span class="rb-tag rb-tag-T" title="Exact tie: same score as {{ (tied_here|length - 1) }} other backend{{ '' if tied_here|length == 2 else 's' }}">tied</span>{% endif %}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
  <p class="opt-small">Cells give each backend’s weighted score for the profile (lower is better) and its rank; the highlighted cell is the profile’s choice. Cells marked <em>tied</em> have exactly the same score (to 12 decimals); the first of them is shown as the choice because of its shorter runtime (tie rule). A profile whose criteria do not separate the backends (for example memory growth at N=100, where it is zero for every backend) is shown as tied. Bias terms are zero: a constant added to every backend’s score cannot change a ranking, and no activation function is needed because all scores are non-negative.</p>
</div>
{% endif %}
<div class="card">
  <div class="card-title">All eligible alternatives: underlying observations and derived scores</div>
  <div class="card-sub">Network size N={{ '{:,}'.format(opt.network_size) }}; scaling exponent fitted across the five measured network sizes (not measured at just one size). Accuracy* is from a separate MNIST run at 60,000 training images, 10 epochs, and hidden width 256. Values below about 15% are approximately chance-level for this 10-class task; the native learning rules are not the same.</div>
  <div class="opt-scroll"><table class="opt-table"><thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Simulator / backend</th><th>Inference (s) ↓</th><th>Peak RSS (MB)</th><th>Memory growth (MB) ↓</th><th>Scaling k ↓</th><th>Fit R²</th><th>MNIST accuracy* (%) ↑</th><th>Weighted score ↓</th><th>Rank sum ↓</th><th>Pareto (active criteria)</th><th>Wins in priority grid</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for r in opt.rows %}<tr class="{{ 'opt-row-winner' if loop.first else '' }}"><td>{{ loop.index }}</td><td><b>{{ r.id }}</b></td><td>{{ '%.5f'|format(r.runtime_s) }}</td><td>{{ '%.1f'|format(r.memory_mb) }}</td><td>{{ '%.1f'|format(r.memory_growth_mb) }}</td><td>{{ '%.3f'|format(r.scaling_k) }}</td><td>{{ '%.2f'|format(r.scaling_r2) if r.scaling_r2 is not none else '—' }}{{ ' ⚠' if r.fit_caution else '' }}</td><td>{{ '%.2f'|format(r.accuracy_pct) if r.accuracy_pct is not none else 'Not available' }}</td><td>{{ '%.3f'|format(r.score) }}</td><td>{{ '%g'|format(r.rank_sum) }}</td><td>{{ 'Yes' if r.pareto else 'No' }}</td><td>{{ r.scenario_wins }}/{{ opt.n_grid_points }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p class="opt-small"><b>Weight interpretation:</b> The initial equal-priority settings are a transparent demonstration, not weights inferred from an established SNN theory, participants or a survey. The recommendation belongs to the current user-chosen scenario. Vary individual priorities and inspect the scenario grid before interpreting it as a robust choice.</p>
  <p class="opt-small"><b>Calculation:</b> {% if opt.normalisation == 'share' %}Share-of-total normalisation: each backend's runtime, memory growth and scaling exponent is divided by the sum over the eligible backends, and accuracy (if weighted) enters as its shortfall from 100% divided by the summed shortfalls, so that a smaller share is better on every criterion.{% else %}Min–max normalisation: inference runtime on a logarithmic scale; memory as the growth in peak process memory above the same backend's footprint at the smallest measured size; the scaling exponent linearly; and accuracy (if weighted) inversely.{% endif %} At N=100 memory growth is zero for every backend, so memory does not discriminate there. The rank sum adds each backend's rank (1 = best) on every active criterion without weights, as a weight-free cross-check. The weighted cost is the sum of four normalised criteria. An absent metric is never substituted with zero. <b>Grid sensitivity:</b> {{ opt.n_grid_points }} equally spaced priority combinations (0%, 25%, 50%, 75%, 100% that sum to 100%). Counts are deterministic sensitivity results, <em>not probabilities</em>. ⚠ marks a scaling fit with R² below 0.80. The Pareto flag compares only metrics carrying nonzero weights.</p>
  <p class="opt-small"><b>Filtered out:</b> {{ opt.excluded.control }} NumPy validation control{{ '' if opt.excluded.control == 1 else 's' }}, {{ opt.excluded.compilation }} compilation-dominated backend{{ '' if opt.excluded.compilation == 1 else 's' }}, {{ opt.excluded.missing }} missing workload sizes, {{ opt.excluded.invalid }} invalid observations, {{ opt.excluded.memory_cap }} over the memory cap, {{ opt.excluded.missing_scaling }} missing scaling fits, {{ opt.excluded.missing_accuracy }} missing accuracy measurements. CV above 15% is flagged in benchmark pages and requires caution when interpreting any close rankings.</p>
  <a class="action" id="multiOptCsvLink" href="/api/optimization.csv?n={{ opt.network_size }}&inference={{ opt.raw_weights.inference }}&memory={{ opt.raw_weights.memory }}&scalability={{ opt.raw_weights.scalability }}&accuracy={{ opt.raw_weights.accuracy }}&norm={{ opt.normalisation }}{{ '&cap=' ~ opt.memory_limit_mb if opt.memory_limit_mb is not none else '' }}">Export current recommendation and all four criteria (CSV)</a>
</div>
{% else %}
<div class="card"><div class="card-title">No eligible recommendation</div><div class="card-sub">{{ opt.notice }}</div></div>
{% endif %}
